WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:07.579
♪ Bulldog, bow wow wow,
our team will never fail ♪

00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:11.701
♪ When the sons of Eli
break through the line ♪

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:15.090
♪ That is the sign we hail ♪

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:19.923
♪ Bulldog, bulldog,
bow wow wow, Eli Yale ♪

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.788
- Good afternoon, we are here today

00:00:27.788 --> 00:00:30.920
to pay tribute to the first women

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.265
of Yale College, 575 trailblazers,

00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:39.038
who came through Phelps Gate in 1969,

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:42.664
and changed this university forever.

00:00:42.664 --> 00:00:45.414
(crowd applauds)

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.130
This is where we find a fitting place

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.423
to honor them, to honor you.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.040
We've chosen this spot

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.590
because it is where many Yale students

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:04.500
historically have begun their Yale careers

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.973
as they move into Old Campus,

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.720
and it is where they march out

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.231
onto the world amidst music
and banners and cheering crowds

00:01:15.231 --> 00:01:17.113
following commencement.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:21.870
And this is not far from
where the first building

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.910
of the collegiate school in New Haven,

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:29.910
now called Yale, was
raised over 300 years ago.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.638
In this place, rich with Yale history,

00:01:33.638 --> 00:01:37.636
we remember that every barrier broken

00:01:37.636 --> 00:01:41.233
is both an end and a beginning.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.020
The first women of Yale College

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.040
inaugurated a new era.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.290
Their legacy is all around us

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.260
in classrooms and in residential colleges,

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.380
in the lives of distinguished
alumni and in the nation

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.383
and world they helped shape.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:06.500
Now, it is represented on our campus.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.840
Today, with the dedication of this

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.260
50th anniversary commemorative stone,

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.930
we honor their courage and commitment.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.230
We remember their spirit and resilience,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.080
your spirit and resilience.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.380
We thank them.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.230
We thank you for being the first.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.300
As we join together with
students, faculty, alumni

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.873
and staff here and around the globe,

00:02:33.873 --> 00:02:38.080
we look with optimism
to future generations

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.240
who will, like the
extraordinary first women

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.310
of Yale College, carry
forward Yale's mission

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.143
of light and truth into the world.

00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:51.620
And now it is a great
privilege to introduce

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.554
my friend, Elizabeth
Alexander, a 1984 graduate

00:02:56.554 --> 00:03:01.554
of Yale College, a prize
winning poet, essayist,

00:03:01.933 --> 00:03:05.150
educator and scholar.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.870
Ms. Alexander is the president

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.696
of the Andrew W. Mellon Foundation.

00:03:09.696 --> 00:03:13.741
She is a former longtime
faculty member at Yale

00:03:13.741 --> 00:03:16.680
where she was the
inaugural Frederick Iseman

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.890
Professor of Poetry and the chair

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.027
of the African-American
Studies Department.

00:03:23.027 --> 00:03:25.870
She received an Honorary Degree of Letters

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.053
from Yale in 2008.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.530
Please join me in welcoming
Elizabeth Alexander.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.510
It is a privilege, once again,

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.436
to welcome you back home.

00:03:37.436 --> 00:03:40.186
(crowd applauds)

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.320
Thank you for doing this.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.530
- Thank you, Peter.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.700
It is such an honor to be here,

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.840
and I know we want to see what's here,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.640
but I am very honored to have been asked

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.310
to deliver some real remarks.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.830
So, it'll take a minute,

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:01.080
but it's a beautiful day.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.293
Meditations on co-education.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.580
I've been thinking about the difference

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.163
between evolution and revolution.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.170
Evolution moves forward in unfolding time,

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.600
and overtime, the organism changes.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.060
There's all the time in
the world to catch up.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.653
That's evolution.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.550
Revolution is a sharp turn, an about face,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.120
occasioning acceleration.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.680
Some things need to be done decisively

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.750
and happen fast.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.690
Sometimes, places and
people and ideas need

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.830
to be jolted forward
into the present tense

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.900
so the organism can continue
to grow and flourish.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.943
Both have their time, place and utility.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.620
Admitting undergraduate women
to Yale was the revolution

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.630
that brought this institution forward.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.270
The rest has been evolutions,
but first the university

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.003
needed to take a sharp
turn into the future.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.010
The first classes of women to attend Yale,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.706
those women that we honor
with this celebration,

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:10.380
were tough-stalked and
bold to use the words

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.990
of the poet Robert Hayden.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.940
Seeds had been planted earlier with women

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.940
who were pioneers in the graduates schools

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:19.698
like Pauli Murray and Grace Hopper.

00:05:19.698 --> 00:05:22.100
I have always felt particularly inspired

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.940
by Otelia Cromwell.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.460
The first black women to earn

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.810
any degree from Yale,
and the first black women

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:31.793
to earn a PhD in English anywhere in 1926.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.540
Her example and history have always felt

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.449
proximate to me because since careers

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:40.070
as professors in schools like Yale

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:43.120
were not open to black women in 1926,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.190
she went onto to teach
at Dunbar High School,

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.140
the renowned black academic high school

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.030
in Washington, D.C.
where one of her students

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.673
was my beloved grandmother, Winona Logan.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.050
I grew up being trained and corrected

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.910
in my language and
grammar and reading habits

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:01.100
by my grandmother who attributed

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.842
every rule to Otelia Cromwell.

00:06:03.842 --> 00:06:05.740
(laughs)

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:07.740
She has been memorialized by Smith College

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.860
where she was the first
black female undergraduate,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.110
and indeed, Smith College

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.580
has an entire Otelia Cromwell day.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:15.990
Though, I dare say, Ms.
Cromwell would disdain

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.653
the fact that on that
day, there are no classes.

00:06:18.653 --> 00:06:19.690
(audience laughs)

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:23.280
And here, at last, there
are not one but two

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.993
portraits of her, just
making sure it sticks.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.350
There isn't a lot on
her in the alumni files,

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.530
but we do have her
dissertation and combing

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.260
the alumni files for any further semblance

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.330
of the women is a letter that she writes

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:41.007
on October 5, 1922 to the
Yale Athletic Association

00:06:41.007 --> 00:06:44.877
requesting two tickets
for the Harvard-Yale game.

00:06:44.877 --> 00:06:48.207
"If I may have the privilege
of buying tickets," she wrote,

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:50.567
"will you send me an application blank?"

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.730
It is noted for alumni
records, that she became

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.932
a member of board of directors
who would have charge

00:06:55.932 --> 00:06:59.640
of what was called "An
Encyclopedia of the Negro."

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.685
A project undergone by W.E.B. Du Bois.

00:07:02.685 --> 00:07:07.685
From 1939 forward, her name is
listed in a special card file

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.190
that I have seen with my eyes.

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:13.704
A card labeled Negroes Who Attended Yale,

00:07:13.704 --> 00:07:15.400
and in other places on the record,

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.800
she's identified as colored.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:19.160
While studying in New Haven,

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.070
Cromwell lived at 65 Edgewood Avenue,

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:24.440
and a letter that she wrote to her father

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.310
summer of 1922 gives a
wonderful description

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.540
of graduate study, but also

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:33.023
of a savvy women scholar making her way.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.010
And to read just a little
bit: Well, I am back

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.600
in New Haven where at least the nights

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.980
are cool after hot days.

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:40.970
I have a large room

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:42.610
facing west with three windows.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.570
In the mornings, I work
in the Yale Library

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:46.851
which is a quiet, roomy
place in the summertime.

00:07:46.851 --> 00:07:51.090
In the afternoons, I work in
my room that is at present

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.600
while I'm making a general
survey of my subject,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:55.810
and it is possible to bring
enough reading material

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:59.913
home from the library to
occupy me for several hours.

00:07:59.913 --> 00:08:03.210
Later on, when my work
becomes more detailed

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:05.640
involving a ceaseless
verification of opinions

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.321
from many sources, I shall
be compelled, I think,

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:11.003
to do all of my reading in the library.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.770
Although, I'm not absolutely certain,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.750
I shall not know for
sure until the department

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.086
approves, I think I
shall work on some faze

00:08:18.086 --> 00:08:20.840
of Elizabethan drama.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.924
For many points of view, it would be

00:08:22.924 --> 00:08:25.420
more comfortable to select a subject

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:27.973
relating to the Negroes,
but two difficulties

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:30.032
stand in the way.

00:08:30.032 --> 00:08:33.310
The improbability of my
being able from what I know

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.770
of the possibilities of
the field to get something

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.490
that would be big enough for the kind

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:39.510
of book I've got to write.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.320
And more importantly,
the fact that any work

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.007
which I might do in that
line would be absolutely

00:08:45.007 --> 00:08:47.830
independent, because
naturally, I'd know more

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:50.025
about it than any of the folks here.

00:08:50.025 --> 00:08:51.410
(laughs)

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.350
In one way, the independent work would

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:55.410
show a certain kind of power,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.330
but on the other hand, my main object

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.910
in being here is to learn scholarly method

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.270
and to benefit by scholarly criticism.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:05.190
Most of all, I want the work I may do

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.618
in the years to come,
if years are granted me,

00:09:07.618 --> 00:09:10.530
to be critically sound.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.190
I am glad that I have a sense of values.

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:16.772
Ambition for place or fame
is not my besetting folly.

00:09:16.772 --> 00:09:20.005
I wish to do work that I like.

00:09:20.005 --> 00:09:23.474
Time to keep unembittered
study and perhaps

00:09:23.474 --> 00:09:25.573
to do this writing some day.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:29.450
That aspiration and making
a place for herself,

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:31.560
a place that was not
supposed to be her place,

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.770
is what the women we celebrate today did

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:35.863
years later in community.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:39.490
I am proud to have arrived
as an undergraduate

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:43.020
in 1980 in the wake of
this first generation

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.213
with their revolutionary
example lighting the way.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:48.700
When my classmates and I arrived,

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.380
it was incredible for us to think

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:52.760
that the first class
with women had graduated

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.500
only a few years before.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.441
To ground us in that moment, 1980 Yale

00:09:58.441 --> 00:10:00.700
didn't have a woman department chair

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:02.590
until three years before we came

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:05.680
nor a female provost, nor a
female president of course.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.610
Whim 'n Rythym played its first concert

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:09.040
when we were undergrads.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.780
The women's sports team captains' pictures

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:15.200
were first place on the wall
of Mory's in the eighties.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.270
The women's studies major was approved

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.091
our sophomore year despite the fact

00:10:19.091 --> 00:10:21.910
that an English professor
submitted petitions

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.480
to the faculty referring to it

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:26.440
as quote, the department of grossness.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:27.313
That is true.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.163
Revolutionary changes
beget natural evolution.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:35.659
You don't know when
you set foot in a place

00:10:35.659 --> 00:10:37.680
if you will belong to it for the rest

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:38.513
of your life.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.280
When you meet someone and are enchanted

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:42.850
and even when you come to love them,

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.400
you cannot know who will
be there for the long road.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.046
You cannot know how the
self who enters a place

00:10:49.046 --> 00:10:51.610
and comes in and then out the other side

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:55.540
is changing, emerging, becoming herself.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.110
You don't know which places

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:59.163
you will forever belong to.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.360
Even the most venerable
and solid institutions,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.510
the most resourced and tradition bound,

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:08.420
are not static.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.906
I love that I could, for example,

00:11:10.906 --> 00:11:13.440
as Yale English major of
any given decade could do,

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.080
recite from the first lines
of the "Canterbury Tales,"

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.450
and that I could do it
together with many of you

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:21.560
and reach back centuries.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:24.713
We won't do it right now,
but we could do it right now.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.970
But here's the point, we belong.

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:31.390
We took those words into our bodies.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:32.260
We held them.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:33.180
We said them.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.540
We believed them as we took joy

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:37.810
in the mastery of memory.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.740
We are all, in part, those words,

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.910
but we are other words too,

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.270
other rituals, other common denominators.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:46.480
We are not one text.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:47.480
We never were.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.610
We belong to an evolving entity

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.410
that believes in the strong spine

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:54.460
of certain traditions,
but also, evolution.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:55.689
Yale has changed.

00:11:55.689 --> 00:11:57.650
You have helped change it.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.693
It will continue to change.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.980
Yale was our place to learn from,

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.470
to demand from, to shout at.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.700
And of our school, I have seen it evolve.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.260
Lucky to come back and teach for 15 years

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.929
and see amazing young
people full of brilliance

00:12:13.929 --> 00:12:16.050
and hope and challenge and verve

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.750
who knew because of the path-breaking work

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.900
of this generation that
they were at an institution

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:26.890
that had survived
revolutions and evolutions,

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:29.390
and that it was their
responsibility to be part

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:31.400
of that dynamic process.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:33.670
And I'm also proud, right now, to have

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:37.556
my own two sons, my
feminists sons, studying here

00:12:37.556 --> 00:12:42.556
unable to imagine that ever
could have gone to school

00:12:42.576 --> 00:12:46.020
without women or been taught only by men

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:49.270
or not read books by all
kinds of different people.

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:51.260
They literally can't imagine it.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:54.747
So, Yale men today are feminists too.

00:12:54.747 --> 00:12:57.497
(crowd applauds)

00:13:00.577 --> 00:13:03.630
I think that evolution is truly possible

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:07.308
when we understand that this
place that was not founded

00:13:07.308 --> 00:13:11.603
to educate the majority
of us is our place too.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:14.620
Together, out of all of our experiences,

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:17.324
we are one alumni body.

00:13:17.324 --> 00:13:20.070
And even though, we have
had moments where we've

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:23.514
been on the cusp of huge
and uncomfortable change,

00:13:23.514 --> 00:13:25.913
this place is ours.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.110
I think that one of the
most important things

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.860
I learned as an undergraduate
at Yale that has guided

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.420
me through my life that I feel that

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:37.038
from this generation,
these Sunflowers, I learned

00:13:37.038 --> 00:13:41.447
is how to be a productive
outsider at a place

00:13:41.447 --> 00:13:44.190
I was inside of.

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:46.950
The gift of being marginalized is that

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:50.420
you have to see kaleidoscopically.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.220
The gift of being marginalized is that

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:54.340
you always know who is not in the room

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:57.003
and how precarious membership can be.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.870
That gift teaches that
bringing others into the room

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:02.690
is our responsibility,

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:05.083
however we may find a way to do it.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:09.750
And so, for the Sunflowers, the classes

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:14.750
of '71, '72, and '73,
I hope these few days

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.190
have been beautiful.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:20.010
I hope they have been full
of somatic remembering.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:23.770
Feeling the first tiny
cool undercurrents of fall

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.010
in New Haven.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.460
Tilting your heads just
so to look up at the tops

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.800
of these buildings in the sunshine.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:33.640
Turning right or left by
instinct when you enter

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:35.410
various buildings.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.728
Being glad that there
are now more bathrooms

00:14:37.728 --> 00:14:39.166
that you can go into.

00:14:39.166 --> 00:14:40.210
(laughs)

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:43.170
Thinking about the good and the bad of it,

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.240
the glory and the struggle.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.790
The vaulting force of what it feels like

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:50.530
to burst into a new stage
of your own development

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:52.413
in a place that never leaves us.

00:14:52.413 --> 00:14:55.610
To which we belong and to
which we will hopefully

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:58.883
all keep coming back as citizens.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.920
We weren't taught by enough women,

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:04.230
but we were taught by legends like

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:07.060
Sylvia Ardyn Boone and Marie Borath.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.033
We didn't read many books
but written by women.

00:15:09.033 --> 00:15:11.180
There weren't enough
of us in the classroom

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:13.530
at the time, but you all came here,

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:14.990
and you left your mark.

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:17.050
And you learned, and
you took that learning

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.110
out into the world, and
you changed this place.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:21.710
And you left your names metaphorically

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:25.910
and now literally on the
walls and on the ground.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.290
Learned what it was to
change an institution

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:31.960
and help it evolve as part
of your responsibilities.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:35.153
And I believe it took a
revolution to do that.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:41.540
I want to close with this
short poem by Muriel Rukeyser.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:45.616
One of the best poets I know
on evolution, revolution,

00:15:45.616 --> 00:15:50.616
and the carefully observed
small moments that make history.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.573
And this poem is called Painters:

00:15:55.510 --> 00:16:00.510
In the cave with a long-ago
flare, a woman stands,

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:02.763
her arms up.

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:07.003
Red twig, black twig, brown twig.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.943
A wall of leaping darkness over her.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:15.163
The men are out hunting
in the early light,

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:19.190
but here in this flicker, one or two men,

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:22.083
painting and a woman among them.

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:27.420
Great living animals
grow on the stone walls,

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:30.921
their pelts, their eyes,
their sex, their hearts,

00:16:30.921 --> 00:16:35.490
and the cave painters
touch them with life,

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:40.490
red, brown, black, a woman
among them, painting.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:43.896
Thank you very much.

00:16:43.896 --> 00:16:46.646
(crowd applauds)

00:17:05.165 --> 00:17:07.915
(crowd chatters)

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:16.831
- Oh.

00:17:16.831 --> 00:17:19.581
(crowd applauds)

00:17:27.606 --> 00:17:28.940
- Thank you:

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:32.810
In September 1969, the first

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:35.870
women undergraduates arrived on campus.

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:39.050
With spirit and determination, these women

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:44.050
of the classes of 1971, 1972, and 1973

00:17:44.863 --> 00:17:49.208
transformed life and
learning in Yale College.

00:17:49.208 --> 00:17:51.958
(crowd applauds)

